::GEO_U03_L07_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, which sides are congruent?
{
=A. Opposite sides#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~B. Adjacent sides only#Adjacent sides are not always congruent in every parallelogram.
~C. Diagonals only#Diagonals have other relationships but are not side pairs.
~D. No sides#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, which angles are congruent?
{
~A. Consecutive angles#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not necessarily congruent.
=B. Opposite angles#Opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.
~C. All exterior angles only#Exterior angles are not the standard relationship used here.
~D. No angles#Parallelograms do have congruent opposite angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, consecutive angles are what?
{
~A. Always congruent#Opposite angles are congruent; consecutive angles are supplementary.
~B. Always 90°#Only rectangles guarantee right angles.
=C. Supplementary#Consecutive angles in a parallelogram sum to 180°.
~D. Always acute#Consecutive angles can vary but must sum to 180°.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
What do the diagonals of a parallelogram do?
{
~A. Stay perpendicular in every case#Perpendicular diagonals are special, not true for every parallelogram.
~B. Have no relationship#There is a defined diagonal relationship.
~C. Create four equilateral triangles#This is not a general parallelogram property.
=D. Bisect each other#Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
If one angle of a parallelogram is 65°, what is the measure of a consecutive angle?
{
=A. 115°#Consecutive angles are supplementary, so 180 - 65 = 115.
~B. 65°#That is the opposite angle, not a consecutive angle.
~C. 90°#A parallelogram is not necessarily a rectangle.
~D. 180°#180° is the sum, not the angle measure.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In an isosceles trapezoid, which angles are congruent?
{
~A. Consecutive interior angles only#Consecutive interior angles along a leg are supplementary.
=B. Base angles on the same base#Base angles of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
~C. All four angles always#All four angles are not always congruent.
~D. No angles#There is a base-angle relationship.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The parallel sides of a trapezoid are called what?
{
~A. Legs#Legs are the non-parallel sides.
~B. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
=C. Bases#The parallel sides of a trapezoid are the bases.
~D. Medians only#A median or midsegment is different from a base.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The non-parallel sides of a trapezoid are called what?
{
~A. Bases#Bases are the parallel sides.
~B. Diagonals#Diagonals connect nonadjacent vertices.
~C. Opposite bases#Opposite bases is not the name of the non-parallel sides.
=D. Legs#The non-parallel sides are the legs.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
The trapezoid midsegment length equals what?
{
=A. The average of the two base lengths#A trapezoid midsegment length is the average of the bases.
~B. The sum of the legs#Leg lengths are not averaged for the midsegment formula.
~C. Half of one base only#Both bases are used.
~D. The product of the bases#The formula uses an average, not a product.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
A trapezoid has bases 8 and 14. What is the midsegment length?
{
~A. 6#This subtracts the bases instead of averaging them.
=B. 11#The midsegment is (8 + 14) / 2 = 11.
~C. 22#This adds the bases but does not divide by 2.
~D. 112#This multiplies the bases.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
A parallelogram has one side of length 12. What is true about the opposite side?
{
~A. It is 6.#There is no halving relationship.
~B. It is 24.#There is no doubling relationship.
=C. It is also 12.#Opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent.
~D. It cannot be related.#The opposite side relationship is known.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
A parallelogram has diagonals that intersect at M. If AM = 7 on diagonal AC, what is MC?
{
~A. 14#14 would be the whole diagonal AC, not MC.
~B. 3.5#3.5 incorrectly halves AM.
~C. Cannot be known#The bisection property gives the value.
=D. 7#Diagonals bisect each other, so AM = MC.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which condition is enough to identify a parallelogram?
{
=A. Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel.#A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel.
~B. Exactly one pair of opposite sides is parallel.#One pair of parallel sides describes trapezoid-related evidence, not necessarily a parallelogram.
~C. Only one angle is acute.#Angle type alone is not enough.
~D. The figure has four sides only.#A four-sided figure is any quadrilateral.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
Which statement about an isosceles trapezoid is true?
{
~A. Its bases are always congruent.#Bases are parallel but not necessarily congruent.
=B. Its legs are congruent.#An isosceles trapezoid has congruent legs.
~C. All sides are congruent.#All sides congruent is not the trapezoid relationship.
~D. It has no base angles.#Trapezoids have base angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
If a trapezoid midsegment is 15 and one base is 12, what is the other base?
{
~A. 3#This subtracts incorrectly.
~B. 27#This adds 12 and 15 without using the average formula.
=C. 18#(12 + x) / 2 = 15, so 12 + x = 30 and x = 18.
~D. 30#This is twice the midsegment, not the missing base.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
In a parallelogram, if one angle is 110°, what is the opposite angle?
{
~A. 70°#70° is a consecutive angle.
~B. 90°#A parallelogram is not necessarily a rectangle.
~C. 180°#180° is the supplementary total for consecutive angles.
=D. 110°#Opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which property helps prove two triangles inside a parallelogram congruent using a diagonal?
{
=A. Opposite sides are congruent and the diagonal is shared.#Opposite side congruence and a shared diagonal can support triangle congruence.
~B. All sides are different.#Parallelograms have opposite side relationships.
~C. Consecutive angles are always equal.#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not always equal.
~D. The diagonal is not part of either triangle.#A diagonal can be a shared side of two triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
In an isosceles trapezoid, if one base angle is 72°, what is the other angle on the same base?
{
~A. 108°#108° may be adjacent along a leg, not the same-base angle.
=B. 72°#Base angles on the same base of an isosceles trapezoid are congruent.
~C. 36°#There is no halving relationship.
~D. 144°#There is no doubling relationship.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which quadrilateral property is specific to parallelograms in this lesson?
{
~A. Only one pair of sides is parallel.#One pair of parallel sides is trapezoid-related.
~B. Base angles are always congruent.#Base angles are a key isosceles trapezoid relationship.
=C. Diagonals bisect each other.#Diagonals bisect each other in parallelograms.
~D. Midsegment equals average of bases.#The midsegment formula is for trapezoids.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
Which relationship belongs to trapezoids?
{
~A. Opposite sides are always congruent.#Opposite sides congruent is a parallelogram property.
~B. Diagonals always bisect each other.#Diagonal bisection is a parallelogram property.
~C. Consecutive angles are always 90°.#Consecutive right angles are not guaranteed.
=D. Midsegment length equals the average of the bases.#The trapezoid midsegment uses the average of the bases.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
If a parallelogram has side lengths labeled x + 3 and 11 on opposite sides, what is x?
{
=A. 8#Opposite sides are congruent, so x + 3 = 11 and x = 8.
~B. 11#This treats x as the full side length.
~C. 14#This adds instead of solving the equation.
~D. 4#This subtracts too much.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
If an isosceles trapezoid has legs 2x and 18, what is x?
{
~A. 16#This does not solve 2x = 18.
=B. 9#The legs are congruent, so 2x = 18 and x = 9.
~C. 20#This adds 2 to 18.
~D. 36#This doubles 18 instead of solving.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
What mistake occurs if a student treats every trapezoid like a parallelogram?
{
~A. They correctly identify all trapezoids.#The assumption is not always correct.
~B. They use the midsegment formula correctly every time.#Midsegment use does not fix the classification error.
=C. They may assume both pairs of opposite sides are parallel when only one pair is guaranteed.#A trapezoid does not necessarily have both pairs of opposite sides parallel.
~D. They prove all sides congruent.#Trapezoids do not necessarily have all sides congruent.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.2
Question:
Which conclusion follows from parallelogram ABCD?
{
~A. angle A ≅ angle B#Consecutive angles are supplementary, not necessarily congruent.
~B. AB ≅ BC#Adjacent sides are not always congruent.
~C. AC ≅ BD in every parallelogram#Diagonals are not always congruent in every parallelogram.
=D. angle A ≅ angle C#Opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.
}

::GEO_U03_L07_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L07_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.3
Question:
Which conclusion follows from an isosceles trapezoid?
{
=A. The diagonals are congruent.#Isosceles trapezoids have congruent diagonals.
~B. The bases are congruent.#Bases are parallel but not necessarily congruent.
~C. Opposite sides are all parallel.#That would describe a parallelogram.
~D. All angles are 90°.#Right angles are not guaranteed.
}


